on the event in the last three editions of the World Championships, was favored to take the inaugural crown. The nation sent " [:a {:href "/athletes/11002"} "Serhiy Petrenko"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/11023"} "Aleksandr Vinogradov"] " to the Games, the latter of whom had earned gold at the two most recent World Championships. The duo won their opening heat, but " [:a {:href "/athletes/10045"} "Tamás Buday"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10056"} "Oszkár Frey"] ", representing 1975 World bronze medalist Hungary, posted a faster time. The Hungarians, in turn, were defeated in the semi-finals by " [:a {:href "/athletes/10513"} "Jerzy Opara"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10481"} "Andrzej Gronowicz"] " of Poland, the 1973 World bronze medalists. Romania’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10580"} "Gheorghe Danielov"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10617"} "Gheorghe Simionov"] ", the 1971 World Champions and 1973 and 1974 runners-up, won a second heat while the Soviet Union took the third. In the final, however, the Soviet resumed their international dominance and won gold nearly two seconds ahead of Poland, who settled for silver. Hungary, meanwhile, had to battle Romania for bronze, which it earned by a margin of only 0.49 seconds. Both the Soviet and Romanian crews would repeat their performance in the C-2 1000, capturing gold and bronze respectively."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "205/211"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/11002"} "Sergey Petrenko"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/11023"} "Aleksandr Vinogradov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]] [:td {} "1:58.22 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] 